# Maps.
#
# Three primitives, and not one of them returns a collection.
# `mapLength` and `mapAt` because a map cannot be taken apart from inside
# the language, and `mapKeyAt` because a fold needs something to walk.
#
# WHAT MADE THE REST OF THIS FILE WRITABLE. `mapKeyAt` — the key at a
# position, or `None` past the end. It is the map's `listAt`, and it is
# the smallest thing that closes the gap: `mapAt` answers a question about
# a key you already have, `mapLength` gives a count with no way to turn a
# count into a key, and a map has no other operation at all. So an
# enumeration has to be primitive; the only choice was how much of one.
# `keys of` was the most — a whole collection, built by the platform, in
# an order the language then had to take on trust. `mapKeyAt` is the
# least: one key, addressed the one way the language already addresses a
# sequence, and every collection over a map is built above it in
# ZDeceptron.
#
# ORDER. A key's position is its insertion order, and that is a promise
# rather than an observation. The runtime value of a `Map of K to V` is a
# JavaScript `Map` and never an object (§5.4), and a `Map` enumerates in
# insertion order for every kind of key where an object does not, for
# integer-like ones. Every mutation builds a new map rather than writing
# into the old one, so a map's order is settled when it is built:
# `["a" to 1, "b" to 2]`, the pair form `[["a",1],["b",2]]` it serialises
# to, and the map rebuilt from those pairs all enumerate `a` then `b`. A
# durable map that came back in a different order would be a build that
# is not reproducible.
#
# COST. `mapKeyAt` is O(1) per call, so every fold below is linear in the
# size of the map: `keys of` and `values of` are both O(n). Each builds
# with `append` in tail position, which is the only shape that stays
# linear — `list.zd` records why.
#
# `atOr` is the operation `examples/leaderboard.zd` could not be written
# without: `sort each player by atOr with table is table, key is
# player.name, fallback is 0`.

foreign mapLength is anywhere
    from "zd:map" as "length"
    takes of value is Map of K to V
    gives Whole

foreign mapAt is anywhere
    from "zd:map" as "at"
    takes value is Map of K to V, key is K
    gives Option of V

foreign mapKeyAt is anywhere
    from "zd:map" as "keyAt"
    takes value is Map of K to V, index is Whole
    gives Option of K

# The keys, in insertion order.
#
# This was a `foreign` until `mapKeyAt` existed, and the reason was not
# the one §17.4.10 gave. Construction had stopped being a blocker when
# `append item to list` landed; what a fold still had no way to get was a
# sequence to walk, and the only sequence a map offered was this
# function's own answer. Writing `keys of` needed exactly the operation
# `keys of` is. It does not any more.
function keys of table
    give keysFrom with table is table, index is 0, taken is empty

function keysFrom with table, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with key
            give keysFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is (append key to taken)

# The values, each in the position of the key it belongs to.
#
# It walks the entries rather than the keys. It used to build the whole
# key list first and walk that, which cost a second collection to produce
# the one that was asked for.
function values of table
    give valuesFrom with table is table, index is 0, taken is empty

function valuesFrom with table, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with key
            when mapAt with value is table, key is key
                None
                    give valuesFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is taken
                Some with value
                    give valuesFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is (append value to taken)

# The target of `table contains key` when `table` is a Map.
function mapContains with table, key
    give isSome of (mapAt with value is table, key is key)

function atOr with table, key, fallback
    give valueOr with maybe is (mapAt with value is table, key is key), fallback is fallback
